tores in this  neighborhood and there's a lot of people  that don't have the resources to either  get there or shop there so we came here  because we knew that this is where the  food resources were needed as well as  the education giving families healthy  food choices they normally can't afford  is perhaps just a band-aid right now but  many hope that America's coronavirus  crisis could finally trigger real policy  change that aims at making  underprivileged communities healthier  more affluent and with that more  resilient to a public health crisis in  the future  and I am joined now by calypso Schalke  du director of the global health policy  think-tank the Center for global  development in London thank you so much  for joining us this afternoon I'm and  I'd like to begin by asking you because  some have called the coronavirus a great  leveler impacting poor and rich alike  what is your response to that sort of  language I'm not so sure you've just  demonstrated using the United States as  an example how the most vulnerable and  poorest amongst the US citizens are the  worst hate and when you look at  countries unfortunately African nations  South Asian nations are much more likely  to be hit the hardest  perhaps not just the direct effects of  the virus but the effects of the global  economic shutdown that the global North  is imposing to manage the outbreak so  with that in mind what would you  recommend I mean what coronavirus  measures you think should be implemented  in less affluent countries and in less  affluent areas within countries  themselves so I think first of all one  size does not fit all I do not think  that we can take the measures imposed in  the United Kingdom or indeed the US or  France Italy and try and implement them  directly in sub-saharan African nations  for all sorts of reasons the African CDC  has issued guidance on  staged rollout of measures for  suppression but I think if we were to  pick the top three or four things  African governments could do first of  all water and sanitation low-tech water  supply we know wash interventions are  very effective and will do good after  the virus is gone in terms of saving  lives secondly when we look at social  distancing it must be done in a way  which takes account of the realities of  poor nations people live in crowded  spaces ninety percent of the people  working work in the informal sector so  these people must go to work all they  will starve to death they and their  family so must consider this as the  starting point and whilst we're talking  about scaling up hospital capacity let's  concentrate again on simple things that  we know save the most lives oxygen  physiotherapy chest physiotherapy  suction that will save lives in normal  hospital settings as opposed to  high-tech ICU ventilators which may well  not be feasible in countries without  electricity for it's simple things that  are in dire need are just briefly before  we go I'd like to ask you because we we  hear a lot about you know the global  economic impact of the corona virus and  I'd like to ask you briefly about how  you see the impact on global poverty in  particular going forward I think it's  likely to be a tragic impact we're  already looking at forecasts of millions  of people falling into poverty and this  reduction in GDP in sub-saharan Africa  which could be up to six percentage  points will directly result in hundreds  of thousands of deaths for instance we  estimate about 300,000 children will not  live to see their first birthday solely  as a result of the projected economic  contraction so this is a direct health  toll of the economic downturn calypso  shakadou of the global health policy  director of global health policy excuse  me at the Center for Global Development  in London thank you thank you

